WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.695
I’m Jerry.

00:00:00.695 --> 00:00:09.657
I own a desktop laser, and Katachi is the tool I wished I had: show it a photo of something you love, and get pieces you can cut.

00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:13.756
It’s a working prototype, built this week on Meta’s models.

00:00:13.956 --> 00:00:17.557
When I got my laser, cutting simple shapes was easy.

00:00:17.557 --> 00:00:22.267
Turning a picture into cut files took hours of tracing and guessing.

00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:25.246
That’s why so many hobby machines sit idle.

00:00:25.246 --> 00:00:29.263
So I brought Meta’s models into a craft I know first-hand.

00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:30.385
Snap a photo.

00:00:30.385 --> 00:00:37.896
Katachi finds the items and layers, cleans them up, lays out sheets for your machine, and guides assembly.

00:00:37.896 --> 00:00:43.211
You can say what you want or tap a button: the same actions, with one undo.

00:00:43.411 --> 00:00:45.565
Example one, a layered cut.

00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:57.055
Katachi lifts the paper cat off the wall and splits it into four colour layers, back to front: gold backer, black cat, white petals, red leaves.

00:00:57.055 --> 00:00:59.927
Each becomes its own smoothed sheet.

00:00:59.927 --> 00:01:02.321
About a minute, for two cents.

00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:05.527
Example two, a collage of crafts.

00:01:05.527 --> 00:01:18.372
From one tray photo it finds twenty-four earrings, matches the pairs by shape and cuts each pair to one size, with repeated holes evened out.

00:01:18.372 --> 00:01:19.921
Under four cents.

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:21.985
Example three, a set.

00:01:21.985 --> 00:01:26.335
Five bookmarks, overlapping and shot at an angle.

00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:36.455
Katachi corrects the perspective, stands each one upright, rebuilds the hidden edges, and makes all five one size.

00:01:36.455 --> 00:01:37.521
Three cents.

00:01:37.721 --> 00:01:43.705
Under the hood: the browser holds the craft, and code does all the geometry.

00:01:43.705 --> 00:01:47.327
A Cloudflare worker keeps the key server-side.

00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:50.791
Muse Spark reads the photo and plans; SAM 3.

00:01:50.791 --> 00:01:51.658
1 outlines.

00:01:51.658 --> 00:01:54.020
Models never emit coordinates.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.947
Every edit is one action with one contract, shared by the chat and the buttons.

00:01:59.947 --> 00:02:02.049
Simple phrases run instantly.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:08.211
Complex requests go to Muse Spark, which plans the steps as a single undoable change.

00:02:08.411 --> 00:02:12.734
To be clear: this is a prototype, not a finished product.

00:02:12.734 --> 00:02:13.871
The core works.

00:02:13.871 --> 00:02:24.109
But traces still follow the photo, hidden fretwork is rebuilt solid, real size is an estimate, and 3D kits from blurry plans are rough.

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:32.590
Next: one clean cut line per piece, a reference card for true millimetres, and real cuts on a real machine.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:38.937
Then Muse Image to imagine hidden detail, and sending jobs straight to the cutter.

00:02:38.937 --> 00:02:40.485
Later, 3D flat-pack.

00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:45.031
Three different crafts, nine cents of Meta model calls.

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:48.903
The hard part, seeing what to cut, already works.

00:02:48.903 --> 00:02:51.906
Build your world, one sheet at a time.

00:02:51.906 --> 00:02:55.067
Try it at katachi dot sensei-hq dot com.
